  "lead": [
   "The Bible is full of rulers. Some kings were asked for and some were warned against. Emperors collected taxes, and councils issued threats. This study teaches one habit for reading all of those passages. Look for two columns: what the ruler is rightly owed, and what belongs to God alone and no ruler may claim."
  ],
  "frame": [
   "In this study, “the state” means government: the people and offices that make laws, collect taxes, keep order and go to war. “Caesar” was the title of the Roman emperor. Jesus was asked whether God’s people should pay his tax, and He answered with two columns at once. The first column is real: there are things a government is owed. The second column is larger, because everything, Caesar included, belongs to God. Most mistakes Christians make about government come from reading only one column. Some treat the ruler as if he were owed everything. Others treat him as if he were owed nothing."
  ],
